    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d4003836-4f5c-5bf0-9bba-b31421e9e64c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186087784/plots/b3308c70830e4fc3d1800890/prompt.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $4$ centered at the point $(-2,4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186087784/plots/b3308c70830e4fc3d1800890/solution.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
